   "text": "A home Wi-Fi network carries a video stream from the internet to a laptop through a series of steps involving the router, access points, radio channels, and packets. When you start streaming a video on your laptop, the request is sent to the internet, and the video data is broken down into small packets. These packets travel through the internet to your home network, where they are received by your router.\n\nThe router acts as a central hub, directing traffic to the appropriate devices. It connects to your internet service provider (ISP) and distributes the internet connection to other devices in your home, including the laptop. The router also manages the allocation of IP addresses to devices on your network.\n\nYour router is connected to an access point, which is often built into the router itself or a separate device. The access point broadcasts a Wi-Fi signal, creating a wireless network that your laptop can connect to. This signal operates on specific radio channels, which are like the frequencies used for radio and television broadcasts. In a home network, these channels are typically in the 2.4 GHz or 5 GHz frequency bands.\n\nWhen your laptop connects to the Wi-Fi network, it sends and receives data using these radio channels. The video stream is transmitted in small packets, each containing a portion of"
